# Keep every checkout byte-for-byte what was committed, on every platform.
#
# Without this, Git for Windows converts text files to CRLF as it checks them out — its
# installer sets core.autocrlf=true system-wide, and that is the git GitHub's windows
# runners use for actions/checkout. The build then runs under MSYS2, whose separate git
# has autocrlf off, so it compares CRLF working files against the LF blobs in the index
# and reports the entire tree as modified.
#
# The symptom was release artifacts named luvie-v0.0.5-dirty-windows-x86_64.* :
# cmake/ProjectVersion.cmake asks `git describe --tags --dirty` at configure time, and got
# "dirty" back for a checkout nobody had touched.
#
# eol=lf rather than text=auto alone, so the answer does not depend on which git reads it.
# Nothing here is committed with CRLF, so this normalises no existing content.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never eligible for conversion. text=auto already detects these by content, but stating
# it means a corrupted image can never be blamed on line-ending handling.
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.dmg   binary
*.zip   binary
